ars | 3 arcminutes (10 800 arcseconds) | Easily resolved; larger than the Moon in the sky | | GN-z11 (one of the most distant galaxies) | 13.4 billion light-years | ~4000 light-years | ~0.004 arcseconds | Resolvable “dot” only because it’s physically huge—thousands of light-years across | | CMB | “Edge” of observable universe (surface of last scattering) | Sky-filling background | 360 degrees | It’s not an object; you don’t resolve it—you measure fluctuations on large angular scales (~1°) | Notice the pattern: the farther we look, the bigger and brighter the things must be for us to see them. A galaxy 13 billion light-years away is visible because it’s roughly **10²⁰ times larger** in physical size than the flag. Distance imposes a hard trade-off: to stay “visible” at greater distances, an object must be proportionally larger or more luminous. A flag doesn’t scale up—it’s always 1.25 metres. --- ### 3. The CMB is the one exception that proves the rule, and it’s not an object You brought up the CMB earlier—rightly—as something we detect from the absolute farthest reaches. But the CMB is **not a distant “thing”**; it’s a relic radiation field that pervades the entire universe. Its brightness per unit angle doesn’t fall off with distance because there is no single distance—it’s the “wall” of the observable universe around us. That’s a completely different physical situation from light emitted by a tiny flag. The CMB is analogous to standing in a room whose walls all glow; you don’t need a telescope to “see” the walls—you just need a detector sensitive to that glow. Flags, planets, and stars are discrete objects whose light gets diluted by the inverse-square law and reduced in angular size as you recede. The CMB doesn’t get smaller or dimmer if you move; it’s everywhere. So the documentaries aren’t wrong to say we see the early universe via the CMB—but they’re not telling you that this “seeing” is more like measuring the temperature of the entire sky than like reading a